  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nPeriods of light rain will persist for portions of the region\nthrough this afternoon with snow mixing in across northern NH\nand northwestern ME. Minor snow accumulations remain possible,\nespecially for elevations of around 1400 ft and above. The \nupper level low that is responsible for this precipitation will\nexit tonight, leaving behind gradually clearing skies and light\nnortherly flow. Low temperatures will be into the 20s to lower \n30s. Surface high pressure will build across the Great Lakes \nregion on Sunday as we remain under broad cyclonic flow from low\npressure located well to our northeast. This upslope flow will \nallow for additional rain and snow shower activity across the \nmtns and towards the Canadian border with mainly dry conditions \nfurther to the south. NW wind gusts may approach 25-30 mph \nduring the afternoon with high temperatures ranging from around \n40 degrees across the north to near 50 south. Any lingering \nprecipitation across the north will end Sunday evening with low \ntemperatures mainly into the 20s areawide.\n\nThere remains strong agreement in latest global and ensemble\nbased guidance that broad high pressure will build across the\neastern half of the CONUS through much of next week. This will\nresult in mainly dry conditions with warm days and cool nights.\nDaytime highs will likely reach the upper 50s to perhaps lower\n60s some afternoons south of the mtns before radiational cooling\nallows for 20-30+ degree diurnal ranges some days.",
